  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 AM CDT Sat Jun 20 2026\n\nActive Weather Potential...\nA few showers may yet linger over northeastern Wisconsin around \nsunrise but clearing skies are expected to quickly work their way \ninto the region by noon. The dry conditions will be short-lived as\nthe next shortwave crosses the region over the afternoon, \ncapitalizing on the lingering low- level moisture and instability \nto develop scattered showers across the area. A few of these \nshowers may also includes some rumbles of thunder, with the best \npotential for any storms being across far northeastern Wisconsin.\nNo severe weather is expected.\n\nSunday, a more potent system will pass well south of the region.\nSlight variations on the track of this system have changed the\nnorthern most extent of precipitation, which has once again made\nfor a dry forecast for our area. If this system begins to track a\nlittle further north once again, some showers may again work their\nway into the forecast.\n\nFinally, active weather continues next week as northwesterly flow\npersists across the region. Another round of widespread showers\nare in the forecast for sometime Tuesday night into Wednesday.\nLong range guidance does support some storm development during\nthis time as well, but its too early to assess potential for\nsevere weather.\n\nTemperatures...\nWithout any major airmass changes, temperatures remain fairly\nsimilar through the forecast period, although there is a slow\nwarming trend through the end of next week. High temperatures are\ngenerally in the 70s but a few areas may see the lower 80s again\nby Friday.",
